1 | backend-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of PR 2791 on branch task/2768-hold-followup, and if that ref does not exist use task slash 2768-followup which is the branch the PR reports. You are not a producer: no commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing. The harness pushes the branch it checks out, so any commit on a producer ref is a production incident. Permitted writes are a slash tmp worktree only. Record the PR head SHA at start and re-check at report; if it moved report BLOCKED with both SHAs. CONTEXT YOU MUST READ FIRST: the comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC 2779 HOLD RETRY INVARIANT. That review returned BLOCK-FIX with three blockers against PR 2779, which merged anyway, and THIS PR is the fix-forward. Your job is to establish whether those three are genuinely closed, and whether closing them opened anything new. THE INVARIANT: keep the traveller authorisation if and only if the client still offers a retry on this row. THE SPECIFIC CLAIMS TO TEST, each by execution rather than by reading the diff. F1: every upstream 4xx outside the enumerated set, specifically 405, 408, 410, 415, 423 and 451, previously fell through with no default arm, mapped to 422, and voided the hold while the client left the CTA retryable. Confirm each of those six now keeps the hold, and confirm the fall-through is closed structurally with an explicit default arm rather than by adding six more cases, because a fix by enumeration will fail again on the seventh shape. F2: the circuit breaker case. ErrCircuitOpen is not a StatusError, so classifyRefusal bailed to the zero verdict and voided while the handler answered 503 and the client stayed retryable. Confirm an open breaker now keeps the hold. This was the dominant real outage path, so verify it by driving the breaker open rather than by reading the new branch. F3: PR 2779 deleted a knownGap escape hatch and claimed there is no longer a legal way to void a hold the client still invites a press against, while the assertion only ranged over eleven enumerated rows. Confirm that claim is now either TRUE across the full shape space or has been narrowed to what it actually covers. A claim that still overstates its coverage is a blocker on its own, independent of the code being right, because the next reader treats it as settled. F4 WAS AN ESCALATION, NOT A BUG, so do not treat it as fixed or unfixed: 401 and 403 now keep the hold, which is the unsafe direction for auth failures since they are not transient and the hold parks until Stripe expiry with the orphan reaper paused. Report what this PR does with 401 and 403 and whether a deliberate decision was recorded, but do not block on it. NEW SURFACE IN THIS PR: it also touches the stays quote step, stay_booking.go, api.yaml and the generated clients. Verify the api.yaml change and the two generated files are consistent with each other and that make generate was actually run rather than the generated files hand edited, since a hand edit here diverges the published contract from the runtime. ALSO: confirm no test was weakened or deleted to make this pass, confirm the new tests fail on the parent commit by reproducing that yourself in a throwaway worktree, and confirm nothing in the diff changes whether an authorisation is CAPTURED as opposed to voided or kept, which is a different irreversible action and is out of scope. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC 2791 HOLD INVARIANT FOLLOWUP, with a verdict of SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE, per-finding severity and file and line citations. No vendor names, emails, booking ids or gcloud payloads. | task/2768-followup
